cff_seed.vsdx is a file written by Microsoft Visio. It is the blank
cross-functional flowchart that Visio creates from its own template, and it
carries Visio's stock shapes (Process, Decision, Start/End, Dynamic connector,
Swimlane). cursus uses it as the starting point for every file it writes, so
the output holds what Visio itself writes rather than a guess at it.

This copy comes from the vsdxkit project
(https://github.com/firmfooting/vsdxkit, tests/fixtures/com_reference/
s05_swimlanes_cfflow.vsdx), BSD 3-Clause, Copyright (c) 2020 Dave Howard,
Copyright (c) 2026 Firm Footing. The stock shapes inside it are Microsoft's.

What Microsoft allows

Microsoft's own wording (support.microsoft.com, "Edit a master shape") is that
you may "distribute drawings that contain them" but may not sell or distribute
the stock shapes themselves.

- Every file cursus writes is a drawing that contains the shapes. Sharing
  those is plainly allowed.
- This seed is also a drawing: it is the starter diagram Visio makes from its
  own template. Its job here, though, is to carry the shapes, which sits
  closer to the line. Many public projects ship such files; none of that is
  legal advice.
- If you would rather not rely on it, start from a drawing saved by your own
  copy of Visio: `cursus build ... --seed my-own.vsdx`. A new flowchart with
  one Process, one Decision, one Start/End and one connector on it is enough.
